[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one parser bug, one fix queued. The reports say
otherwise. Read by tenant, booking form, channel and what the body shows, they hold three
problems. Each one has a ticket of its own:

- [#1 `parser`](https://github.com/jwegrzyn/mega-what-it-shows/issues/1): day-first dates are read month first.
- [#2 `hint`](https://github.com/jwegrzyn/mega-what-it-shows/issues/2): the booking-v3 form tells day-first tenants to type `MM/DD`.
- [#3 `shift`](https://github.com/jwegrzyn/mega-what-it-shows/issues/3): bookings move by one day between time zones.

<Callout>
The tickets are the source of truth for status, outcome and owner, so this page keeps none of
them. To know where a problem stands, read its ticket:
<code>gh issue view 3 -R jwegrzyn/mega-what-it-shows</code>, or
<a href="https://github.com/jwegrzyn/mega-what-it-shows/issues?q=is%3Aissue">all three tickets</a>.
New reports go to the ticket of their problem, not to #4471.
</Callout>

## The split

The desk's own count. The `parser` row is ticket #1 and the `hint` row is ticket #2. The note
under the table is ticket #3: those reports are counted under `parser` by tenant, as the desk
files them, but the parser fix does not stop them.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## How to tell them apart

- **`parser`**: the body shows day and month swapped (7 April → 4 July). The tenant writes
  the day first and the report came by app or email, or by form on booking-v2.
- **`hint`**: the same swap, but sent through the booking-v3 form by a day-first tenant.
  booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field, people type what it says, and the form
  reads it the tenant's way. The parser fix does not touch this.
- **`shift`**: no swap at all. The date moves by one day (Tuesday 12 May → Monday 11 May),
  differs between clients in Lisbon and Toronto, or the invite arrives before the booking.
  That is a time-zone conversion, not date order. These are the one-day moves in
  `desk-notes.json`. The parser fix does not stop them; they belong in #3.

## Why it was merged as one

- Intake tags by the reporter's browser, not the tenant: every report from an `en-US` browser
  was tagged `hint`, every other one `parser`. The "tagged as" column shows how far that is
  from the real split.
- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject. These reports have
  different subjects and still landed in one ticket, and the subject says nothing about the
  cause. Merge on cause: tenant locale, booking form, channel, and what the body shows.
- The export repeats reports across overlapping pages. Count by id; the table does.

